\begin_layout Subsection
Product split
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We default to fixed shares of products for each final use:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{p,u}=\mu_{p,u}U_{u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The shares can be endogenized by other modules to allow substitution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Country of origin split
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We split demand by country of origin:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{p,u,o}=\mu_{p,u,o}U_{p,u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
o=\left\{ \text{domestic},\text{import}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The equilibrium condition that is links domestic production with demand is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{p,u}=U_{p,u,\text{domestic}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Import-substitution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To introduce import-substitution in a submodule,
 endogenize 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,u,o}$
\end_inset

.
 Substitution by product,
 rather than also by use,
 might be enough.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{p,u,o}=\hat{\mu}_{p,u,o}\left(\frac{P_{p,o}+t_{p,o}}{P_{p}^{U}+t_{p}^{U}}\right)^{-E_{p}}U_{p,u}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Production (cost minimization)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the output to be produced,
 the production sub-model calculates a marginal cost of production and the demand for each input factor into production.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Production function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We assume a nested CES production function with
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{factors of production}=k\cup r\cup l
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 are types of capital,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 are types of intermediate inputs,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 are types of labor (with one type being perfectly fine).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f=\text{factors of production}\cup\text{F}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Demand for the factor or nest 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{f}$
\end_inset

 in a specific industry (we omit the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 index here) is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X_{i,f}=\mu_{i,f}X_{F}\left(\frac{p_{f}}{p_{F}}\right)^{E_{F}}|f\in F
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where F is a production nest and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{f}$
\end_inset

 is the usercost of the production factor or nest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The usercost of a nest is given by the budget constraint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{F}X_{F}=p_{f1}X_{f1}+p_{f2}X_{f2}|F=\left\{ f1,f2\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Usercost of factors of production can either be simple spot prices,
 or come from complicated usercost expressions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We implement the nesting structure very compactly,
 using a single variable and demand equation for all factors of production,
 letting a mapping handle the nesting structure.
 The main upside is that this enables us to vary the nesting structure depending on the industry.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Capital adjustment costs and other frictions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Various frictions can should be added to the simple usercost expressions,
 e.g.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{f=\text{labor}}=\text{wage}+\text{matching friction}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The friction itself should be detailed in a satellite model,
 not in the production module.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Output and marginal cost
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Net output is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y=X_{F=\text{TopNest}}-\text{friction losses}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Friction losses can for example be foregone output due to capital adjustment costs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The marginal cost of production is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MC\cdot Y=P_{\text{F=TopNest}}Y+\text{production taxes}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Multiple types of capital
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This can be changed in submodules,
 for example by splitting capital investments (gross capital formation) into multiple types of fixed assets,
 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{K}=\sum_{k}I_{k}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
I_{k}=\sum_{i}I_{k,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Endogenize 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,K}$
\end_inset

 by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{p,K}=\sum_{k}I_{p,k}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each type of capital,
 we assume fixed product shares.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
I_{p,k}=\mu_{p,k}I_{k}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $I_{k,i}$
\end_inset

 is given by cost-minimizing a production function subject to a capital accumulation laws of motion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Multiple types of intermediate inputs (energy)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can split use in production into different types of intermediate inputs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{p,i}=\sum_{r}\mu_{p,r,i}R_{r,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where for example
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r=\left\{ \text{transport energy},\text{heating},\text{processing energy},\text{other intermediates}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{p,re,i}$
\end_inset

 is given by cost-minimizing a production function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming same product shares for energy inputs across industries is not needed and does not simplify much.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Pricing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Markups in basic prices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We replace our standard model of monopolistic competitions with fixed markups by 
\emph on
industry 
\emph default
with a model of fixed markups by 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $\text{product}\times\text{use}$
\end_inset


\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our fundamental assumption is that the marginal cost of producing a product is independent of how the product is used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The marginal cost of producing a product is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{\emph{MC}}_{p}Y_{p}=\sum_{i}\text{\emph{MC}}_{i}Y_{p,i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The basic price by of a product is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{p,u}^{Y}=\theta_{p,u}\text{\emph{MC}}_{p}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{p,u}$
\end_inset

 is the markup parameter (resulting from monopolistic competition.
 It can be endogenized to model price rigidity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Trade margins
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A subset of products are have little or no direct demand,
 but are necessary costs added to the price of other products - these are called trade (and transport) margins.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
it=\left\{ \text{wholesale services},\text{retail services},\text{transport services}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The user price of a product is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{p,u}^{U}U_{p,u}=\sum_{o}\left(P_{p,o}+t_{p,u,o}+m_{p,u,o}\right)U_{p,u,o}\left(1+t_{p,u,o}^{\text{VAT}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{p,u,o}$
\end_inset

 are net product taxes and subsidies,
 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p,u,o}$
\end_inset

 are trade margins,
 and finally 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{p,u,o}^{\text{VAT}}$
\end_inset

 are net 
\emph on
ad valorem
\emph default
 taxes (it is convenient to keep these separate from other net duties,
 as they typically also apply to the trade margin).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The real demand for trade margins for each use is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{u}^{m}=\sum_{o,p}m_{p,o,u}Y_{p,o}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is translated into demand for trade margin products using fixed shares pr.
 use
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{it,u}^{m}=m_{it,p,u}U_{p,u}^{m}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Households
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The household module determines total demand for private consumption (consumption savings decision).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Consumption savings decision
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our default model is a household maximizing CRRA utility with habits,
 a hand-to-mouth share of consumption,
 and utility of wealth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Private consumption types
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can split total private consumption into multiple groups.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Endogenize 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{p,C}$
\end_inset

 by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{p,C}=\sum_{c}C_{p,c}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c=\text{consumption groups}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{p,c}=\mu_{p,c}C_{c}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $C_{c}$
\end_inset

 is given by optimizing consumption bundle of CES nest utility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Exports
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can model export demand directly for domestic production of each product,
 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{p,X}$
\end_inset

,
 making sure to use prices that include logistics and trade margins.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Imports for re-export,
 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{p,X,\text{imports}}$
\end_inset

,
 are completely exogenous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Government expenditure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Determines demand for public consumption and government transfers to other sectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Taxes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Tax accounting and marginal tax rates (derivatives of total revenues wrt.
 to various tax bases).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ideally,
 other modules should only use aggregate marginal tax rates and aggregate tax revenues.
 E.g.,
 a household budget contains a total taxes paid variable,
 while a labor market decision equation uses only a marginal tax on labor income.
 The break down of marginal taxes into income brackets etc.
 is kept in the taxes module.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Labor market
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Simplest version is a forward-looking Phillips curve with exogenous labor supply.
\end_layout

